# ---------------------------------------------------------------- DPDP and localisation
ARTICLES.append(article(
    'dpdp-data-localisation-data-centres-india',
    'DPDP Act and data localisation: what it means for data centres in India (2026)',
    "India's DPDP Act 2023 restricts transfers by a negative list, not blanket localisation. RBI, SEBI, IRDAI, CERT-In and telecom licences do require data in India. What each means for data centres.",
    'India',
    'The Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023 does not require all personal data to stay in India. Section 16 lets the central government block transfers to countries it notifies, and keeps stricter sector rules in force. The DPDP Rules 2025, announced on 14 November 2025, phase in over 18 months to May 2027 and let the government require significant data fiduciaries to keep specified data in India. Hard localisation already comes from sector rules: RBI for payment data since 2018, SEBI for regulated entities’ cloud data since 2023, IRDAI for insurance-sector data and logs, and CERT-In for logs. Those rules, more than the DPDP Act, put data center demand on Indian soil today.',
    [
        'DPDP Section 16 is a negative list: transfers are allowed unless a country is notified.',
        'Most DPDP Rules obligations apply from May 2027.',
        'RBI requires the entire payment system data to be stored only in India.',
        'SEBI regulated entities must keep cloud data, including DR and logs, within India.',
        'CERT-In requires 180 days of ICT logs kept within India and five years of customer records at data centres.',
    ],
    [
        sec('rules-table', 'Which rules keep data in India',
            table(['Rule', 'Who it binds', 'What must stay in India', 'Since'], [
                ['RBI circular of 6 April 2018', 'Payment system providers', 'The entire data relating to payment systems, including end-to-end transaction details', 'Compliance due October 2018'],
                ['SEBI cloud framework of 6 March 2023', 'SEBI regulated entities', 'Data and processing, including DC, DR, near-DR and logs; PaaS and SaaS on MeitY-empanelled data centres with a valid STQC audit', '2023'],
                ['IRDAI Information and Cyber Security Guidelines, 24 April 2023', 'Insurance-sector regulated entities', 'ICT infrastructure logs, critical and business data (entities other than insurers); insurers under record-keeping regulations', '2023, amended April 2026'],
                ['CERT-In directions of 28 April 2022', 'Service providers, data centres, body corporates and government', 'ICT logs for a rolling 180 days within Indian jurisdiction', '2022'],
                ['Unified Licence clause 39.23(viii)', 'Telecom licensees', 'Subscriber accounting information and user information, with listed exceptions', 'Licence condition'],
                ['DPDP Act Section 16', 'All data fiduciaries', 'Nothing by default; transfers to notified countries can be blocked', 'Act of 11 August 2023'],
                ['DPDP Rules 2025', 'Significant data fiduciaries', 'Personal data the government specifies on a committee’s recommendation', 'From May 2027'],
            ], 'rbi', 'sebi', 'irdai-cam', 'certin', 'dot-ul', 'dpdp-act', 'pib-dpdp', 'taxmann-loc'),
        ),
        sec('dpdp-act', 'What the DPDP Act says about cross-border transfer',
            p('Section 16(1) of the Act says the central government "may, by notification, restrict the transfer of personal data by a Data Fiduciary for processing to such country or territory outside India as may be so notified." Section 16(2) keeps any other law that provides "a higher degree of protection for or restriction on transfer", which preserves RBI, SEBI and IRDAI localisation.', 'dpdp-act'),
            p('Section 10 lets the government notify significant data fiduciaries on factors including the volume and sensitivity of data, risk to data principals, sovereignty and integrity of India, electoral democracy, security of the State and public order. They must appoint a Data Protection Officer based in India and an independent data auditor, and run periodic impact assessments and audits. The highest penalty in the Schedule is ₹250 crore, for failing to take reasonable security safeguards.', 'dpdp-act'),
        ),
        sec('dpdp-rules', 'The DPDP Rules 2025 and the May 2027 date',
            p('MeitY announced the DPDP Rules 2025 on 14 November 2025 with an 18-month phased compliance timeline. Consent managers must be Indian companies, data principal requests must be answered within 90 days, and significant data fiduciaries "must also comply with government-specified restrictions on certain categories of data, including localisation where required."', 'pib-dpdp'),
            p('Under the Rules, the government can specify, on a committee’s recommendation, personal data that a significant data fiduciary must process subject to the restriction that it and the traffic data about its flow are not transferred outside India.', 'taxmann-loc'),
            p('In January 2026 Business Standard reported that MeitY was considering cutting the compliance period for key rules from 18 to 12 months. We found no amendment notified, and no significant data fiduciary or restricted-country list notified, as of 21 November 2026.', 'bs-dpdp'),
        ),
        sec('sector-rules', 'Sector rules that already require data in India',
            p('RBI’s circular of 6 April 2018 requires all system providers to ensure that "the entire data relating to payment systems operated by them are stored in a system only in India", including full end-to-end transaction details. The foreign leg of a transaction may also be stored abroad.', 'rbi'),
            p('SEBI’s cloud framework of 6 March 2023 says "the data should reside/be processed within the legal boundaries of India", covering DC, DR, near-DR and logs. For PaaS and SaaS, the provider must store or process regulated entity data only within data centres empanelled by MeitY and holding a valid STQC audit status.', 'sebi'),
            p('IRDAI’s guidelines of 24 April 2023 require regulated entities other than insurers to store all ICT infrastructure logs, critical and business data in India. Insurers keep primary data in India under separate record-keeping regulations. IRDAI amended the guidelines on 6 April 2026; confirm whether the localisation clause changed.', 'irdai-cam', 'irdai-2026'),
            p('CERT-In’s directions of 28 April 2022 require 6-hour incident reporting and ICT logs kept for a rolling 180 days within Indian jurisdiction. Data centres, VPS, cloud and VPN providers must also keep validated subscriber records for five years or longer.', 'certin'),
            p('Telecom licensees may not transfer subscriber accounting information or user information outside India, with exceptions for roaming and certain subscribers, under clause 39.23(viii) of the Unified Licence.', 'dot-ul'),
        ),
        sec('meaning', 'What this means for data centre demand and design',
            ul([
                'Banks, payment companies, insurers and securities firms need primary and disaster recovery capacity inside India, often in two different seismic and flood zones.',
                'Operators that want SEBI-regulated PaaS and SaaS workloads need MeitY empanelment and STQC audit status for the facility.',
                'Log retention, incident reporting and customer records are operating duties for the data centre itself, not only for its tenants.',
                'The DPDP Act alone does not force localisation today. Watch for significant data fiduciary notifications and any restricted-country list from May 2027.',
            ], 'sebi', 'certin', 'dpdp-act'),
            p('This page summarises public rules and is not legal advice.'),
            invariant('India'),
        ),
    ],
    [
        faq('Does the DPDP Act require data localisation in India?', 'Not by default. Section 16 allows transfers unless the government notifies a country as restricted, and keeps stricter sector rules such as RBI’s in force.', 'dpdp-act'),
        faq('When do the DPDP Rules 2025 come into force?', 'In phases over 18 months from November 2025. Most obligations, including those on significant data fiduciaries, apply from May 2027.', 'pib-dpdp'),
        faq('Do payment companies have to store data in India?', 'Yes. Under RBI’s 2018 circular the entire payment system data must be stored only in India.', 'rbi'),
        faq('Can SEBI regulated entities use cloud data centres outside India?', 'No. SEBI’s 2023 framework requires the data, including DR and logs, to reside and be processed within India.', 'sebi'),
        faq('What does CERT-In require from a data center in India?', 'Incident reports within 6 hours, ICT logs for 180 days kept within India, and validated subscriber records for at least five years.', 'certin'),
        faq('Does a data centre need MeitY empanelment?', 'Empanelment is MeitY’s process for cloud service offerings, audited by STQC. SEBI’s framework makes it a condition for PaaS and SaaS providers serving regulated entities.', 'sebi'),
    ],
    srcs('rbi', 'sebi', 'irdai-cam', 'irdai-2026', 'certin', 'dot-ul', 'dpdp-act', 'pib-dpdp', 'taxmann-loc', 'bs-dpdp'),
    ['national-data-centre-policy-india', 'data-centre-incentives-india-central', 'data-centre-approvals-india', 'data-centre-companies-india', 'data-centres-in-india'],
    minutes=8,
))

